<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anubanini</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anubanini was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lullubi">Lullubi</a>, a mountain people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>, in the late third millennium BCE. He is known from a rock relief at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sar-e-pol-e-zahab">Sar-e Pol-e Zahab</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, showing him standing over defeated enemies before the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> (Ishtar), in a style copying Akkadian royal monuments. An Akkadian inscription with the relief records his victories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anubanini.jpg" alt="Anubanini" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Koorosh Nozad Tehrani · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anubanini_relief_constituents_King_Anubanini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
